80/20 Rule
A higll percentage of effects in any large system are
ca used by a low percentage of variables.'                                                                                                                             OlMiP

                                                                                                                                                  New Blank Document
The 80/20 rule asserts that approximately 80 percent of the effects generated by           I   Also known as Pareto's Principle, Juran's          Open ...
any large system are caused by 20 percent of the va riables in thai system. The                Pnnciple, imd Vital Few and Trivial Many Rule.     Open Web Page ...
80/20 rule is observed in all large systems, including those in economics, manage·                                                                Close                  lMiW
ment, user-interface design, quality control, and engineering, to name a few. The          , The first recognition of the 80/20 rule is attrib-
specific percentages are not important, as measures of actual systems indicate that          uted to Vilfredo PareLo, an Italian economist        Save                   lMiS
                                                                                             who observed that 20 percent of the Italian          Save As ...
the proportion varies between 10 percent and 30 percent. The universality of the             people possessed 80 percent of the wealth.
80/20 rule suggests a link to normally distributed systems, which limits its application     The seminal work on the 8CV2O rule Is QualJly        Page Setup .
to variables that are influenced by many small and unrelated effects----e.g., systems        Control Handbook by Joseph M. Juran (Ed.),
that are used by large numbers of people in a variety of ways. A few examples of             McGraw-Hill, 1951.

the 80120 rule include: 7

     80 percent of a product's usage involves 20 percent of its features.
     80 percent of a town's traffic is on 20 percent of its roads.
     80 percent of a company's revenue comes from 20 percent of its products.
     80 percent of innovation comes from 20 percent of the people.
     80 percent of progress comes from 20 percent of the effort.
     80 percent of errors are caused by 20 percent of the components.

The 80/20 rule is useful for focusing resources and, in turn, realizing greater
efficiencies in design. For example, if the critical 20 percent of a product's features
are used 80 percent of the time, design and testing resources should focus
primarily on those features. The remaining 80 percent of the features should be
reevaluated to verify their value in the design. Similarly, when redesigning systems
to make them more efficient, focusing on aspects of the system beyond the critical
20 percent quickly yields diminishing returns; improvements beyond the critical 20
                                                                                                                                                                                82 2A·UPOD qx
percent will result in less substantial gains that are often offset by the introduction
of errors or new problems into the system.

All elements in a design are not created equaL Use the 80/20 rule to assess the
value of elements, target areas for redesign and optimization, and focus resources
efficiently. Noncritical functions that are part of the less-important 80 percent should
be minimized or even removed altogether from the design. When time and resources
are limited, resist efforts to correct and optimize designs beyond the critical 20 per-
cent, as such efforts yield diminishing returns. Generally, limit the application of
the 80/20 rule to variables in a system that are influenced by many small and
unrelated effects.

See also Cost-Benefit, Form Follows Function, Highlighting, and Normal Distribution.




