                                                                                             II" [laws of Organization in Perceptual Formsl
set of individual elements as a single, recognizable pattern, rather than multiple,                                                           Ulcn as individual elements.
                                                                                             by Max Wertheimer, PsychoJogische
individual elements. The tendency to perceive a single pattern is so strong that             Forschung. 1923. vol. 4, p. 301-350, reprinted
people will close gaps and fill in missing information to complete the pattern if            in A Source Book of Geslil/( Psychology by
necessary. For example, when individual line segments are positioned along a                 Willis O. Ellis (edJ, Routledge & Kegan Paul.
circular path, they are first perceived holistically as a circle, and then as compris-       1999. p. 71-88.
ing multiple, independent elements. The tendency to perceive information in this
way is automatic and subconscious; it is likely a function of an innate preference       l   See, for example, Understandmg Comics: The
