Confirmation using a dialog involves establishing a verbal interaction with the
person using the system. It is most commonly represented as a dialog box on a
software display (e.g., "Are you sure you want to delete all files?"). In this method.
dialog boxes directly ask Ihe user if the acUon was intended and if they would like
to proceed. Confirmations should be used sparingly, or people will learn to ignore                                                                                                     You have nat entered a subject. It Is re(ommended
                                                                                                                                                                                       that all menages ha ve subjects. Would you like to
them, and become frustrated at the frequent interruption. Dialog messages should                                                                                                                                                                            'N'
                                                                                                                                                                                       send the message anyway?
be concise but detailed enough to convey accurately the implications of the action.
The message should end with one question that is structured to be answered Yes or
No, or wilh an action verb that conveys the action to be periormed (the use of OK                                                                            -, I                        o      Pon' t . ho w thl, ma uoll a olloln

                                                                                                                                                                                                                                I~-C-an-'.-I~I I   Send I
and Cancel should be avoided for confirmations). For less critical confirmations that
act more as reminders, an option to disable the confirmation should be provided.

Confirmation using a two-step operation involves a preliminary step that must occur
 prior to the actual command or input. This is most often used with hardware
controls, and is often referred to as an armlfire operation-first you arm the compo-
nent, and then you fire (execute) it. For example, a switch cover might have to be
lifted in order to activate a switch, two people might have to turn two unique keys in                                                          Common exampk5 of confirmation
order to launch a nuclear VoJeapon, or a control handle in a spacecraft might have                                                              strategies Include: typing in a pass-
to be rotated and then pushed down in order to be activated. The purpose of the                                                                 word twice to confirm spelling:
two-step operation is to prevent accidental activation of a critical control. If the oper-                                                      confirming the intent of an action by
ation works only when the two-step sequence has been completed. it is unlikely                                                                  chcklng tile action button (Senti) With
that the operation will occur accidentally. Two-step operations are commonly used                                                               an option to disable future confirma-
for critical operations in aircraft, nuclear power plants, and other environments                                                               tions; haVing to remove a lock to open
involving dangerous operations.                                                                                                                 a valve; and having to turn two unique
                                                                                                                                                key~ to complete a launch CircUit.

Use confirmations to minimize errors in the performance of critical or Irreversible
operations. Avoid overusing confirmations to ensure users do not begin to ignore
them. Use a two-step operation for hardware confirmations, and a dialog box for
software confirmations. Permit less critical confirmations to be disabled after an
initial confirmation.

See also Constraint, Errors, Forgiveness, and Garbage In-Garbage Out.

