Negative feedback loops are effective for resisting change. For example, the                                                               Negative Feedback Loop
Segway Human Transporter uses negative feedback loops to maintain equilibrium.
As a rider leans forward or backward, the Segway accelerates or decelerates to
keep the system in equilibrium. To achieve this smoothly, the Segway makes one
hundred adjustments every second. Given the high adjustment rate, the oscilla-
tions around the point of equilibrium are so small as to not be detectable.
However, if fe'Ner adjustments were made per second, the oscillations would
increase in size and the ride lNGuld become increasingly jerky.                                                                                                                   /
A key lesson of feedback loops is that things are connected-changing one vari-
able in a system will affect other variables in that system and other systems. This
                                                                                                                                                                              I                             Angle of Segway
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     -             Velocity


is important because it means that designers must not only consider particular
elements of a design, but also their relation to the deSign as a whole and the
greater environment. Consider positive feedback loops to perturb systems to
change, but include negative feedback loops to prevent runaway behaviors that
lead to system failure. Consider negative feedback loops to stabilize systems, but
be cautious in that too much negative feedback in a system can lead to stagnation.'                                                                                 Ti me

