Fibonacci patterns are found in many classic works, including classic poetry, art,    , See, for example, Structural Patterns and
music, and architecture. For example, it has been argued that Virgil used Fibonacci     Proportions in Virgil's Aeneid by George Eckel
sequences to structure the poetry in the Aeneid. Fibonacci sequences are found in         Duckworth, University of Michigan Press,
                                                                                          1952; and "Did Mozart Use the Golden
the musical compositions of Mozart's sonatas and Beethoven's Fifth Symphony. Le
                                                                                          Section?" by Mike May, American Scientist,
Corbusier meshed key measures of the human body and Fibonacci sequences to                March-April 1996; and Le Modular by Le
develop the Modulor, a classic system of architectural proportions and measure-           Corbusier, Birkhauser, 2CXX) [19481 .
ments to aid designers in achieving practical and harmonious designs.2
                                                                                      l   "All That Glitters: A Review of Psychological
Fibonacci sequences are generally used in concert with the golden ratio, a prin -         Research on the Aest hetics of the Golden
ciple to which it is closely related. For example, the division of any two adjacent       Section" by Christopher D. Green, Perception,
                                                                                          1995, vol. 24, p. 937- 968.
numbers in a Fibonacci sequence yields an approximation of the golden ratio.
Approximations are rough for early numbers in the sequence but increasingly
accurate as the sequence progresses. As with the golden ratio, debate continues
as to the aesthetic value of Fibonacci patterns. Are such patterns considered
aesthetic because people find them to be more aesthetic or because people have
been taught to believe they are aesthetic? Research on the aesthetics of the
golden ratio tends to favor the former, but little empirical research exists on the
aesthetics of non-golden Fibonacci patterns. 3

The Fibonacci sequence continues to be one of the most influential patterns in
mathematics and design. Consider Fibonacci sequences when developing interest-
ing compositions, geometric patterns, and organic motifs and contexts, especially
when they involve rhythms and harmonies among multi ple elements. 00 not
contrive designs to incorporate Fibonacci sequences, but also do not forego oppor-
tunities to explore Fibonacci relationsh ips when other aspects of the design are
not compromised.

See also Aesthetic-Usability Effect, Golden Ratio, and Most Average Facial
         Appearance Effect.




