The Gu tenberg diagram divides a display medium into four quadrants: the primary
optical area at the top left, the terminal area at the bottom right, the strong fallow
                                                                                           I   Also known as the Gutenberg rule and the Z
                                                                                               pattern of processing.
                                                                                                                                              diagram rtoes Ilot apply, and can con-
                                                                                                                                              strain COn1IJOsltlon unnecessarily.                                           ~~1;;iiE§lt
area at the top right, and the weak fallow area at the bottom left. Accord ing to the
diagram, Western readers naturally begin at the primary optical area and move              I   The seminal work on the Gutenberg diagram Is
                                                                                               attributed 10 the typographer Edmund Arnotd,
across and down the display medium in a series of sweeps to the terminal area.
                                                                                               who is said 10 have developed the concept in                Primary Optical Area
Each sweep begins along an axis of orientation- a horizontal line created by aligned           the 1950s. See, for example, Type & Layout:
elements, text lines, or explicit segments- and proceeds in a left-to-right direction.         How Typography and Design Can Get Your                                      Strong Fallow Area
The strong and weak fallow areas lie outside this path and receive minimal atten-              Message Across or Get in the Way. by Colin
tion unless visually emphasized. The tend ency to follow this pa th is metaphorically          Wheildon, Strathmoor Press. 1995.

attributed to reading gravity- the left-nght, top-bottom habit formed from reading.'

Designs that follow the diagram work in harmony with reading gravity, and return
readers to a logical axis of orientation, purportedly improving reading rhythm and
comprehension. For example, a layout following the Gutenberg diagram would
place key elements at the top left (e.g .. headline), middle (e.g., image), and bot-
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       ,   '~.--'.'-
