Life Cycle
                                                                                                                                                                  Introduction      Growth                         Maturity                     Decline
All products progress sequentially through four stages of
existence: introduction, growth, maturity, and decline.                                                                                                       +

                                                                                                                                                                                                           /
All products progress through stages of existence that roughly correspond with             1   The seminal \¥Ofk on the producllife cycle is                                                           /

birth, adolescence, adulthood. and death. For example. a new type of electronic                "Internationallnvestmenl and International
                                                                                               Trade in the Product Cycle" by Raymond
                                                                                                                                                                                                   /
device is envisioned and developed; its popularity grows; after a while its sales
                                                                                               Vernon, Quarterly Journal of Economics, 1966,                                                   /
plateau; and then finally, the sales decline. Understanding the implications of each                                                                                                       /
                                                                                               vol. 80, p. 190-207. A contemporary review of
                                                                                                                                                                                       /
of these stages allows designers to prepare for the unique and evolving require-               the product life cycle is found in Marketmg
ments of a prcxiuct over its lifetime. There are four basic stages of life for all proo-       Management by Philip Kotler, Prentice-Hail,
                                                                                               11" ed., 2002.
ucts, introduction, growth, maturity, and decline.'

Introduction
The introduction stage is the official birth of a product. It will at times overlap with
the late testing stage of the development cycle. The design focus in this stage is to
monitor early use of the design to ensure proper performance, \YOrking closely with                                                            Audience           Early Adopters   Mainstream                    Late Adopters                  Laggards
customers to tune or patch the design as necessary.
                                                                                                                                               Market                 Small          Growing                         Large                     Contracting
Growth
The growth stage is the most challenging stage, where most products fail. The                                                                  Sales                   Low            High                         Flattening                   Moderate
design focus In this stage is to scale the supply and performance of the product to
meet the growing demand, and provide the level of support necessary to maintain                                                                                        Low          Moderate                         High                       Moderate
                                                                                                                                               Competition
customer satisfaction and growth. Efforts to gather requirements for the next-gen-
eration product should be underway at this stage.
                                                                                                                                               Business Focus       Awareness      Market Share                Customer Retention               Transition

Maturity
                                                                                                                                               Design Focus          Tuning          Scaling                        Support                     Transition
The maturity stage is the peak of the product life cycle. Product sa les have begun
to diminish and competition from competitors is strong. The design focus at this
stage is to enhance and refine the product to maximize customer satisfaction and
                                                                                                                                                                                                                        The needs of a product change over
retention. Design and development of the next-generation procluct should be well
                                                                                                                                                                                                                        the course of ,ts hfe cycle. It '5 impor-
underway at this stage.
                                                                                                                                                                                                                        !ant to understand the dynamiCs of
                                                                                                                                                                                                                        these changes In order to focus bUSI-
Decline                                                                                                                                                                                                                 ness and design resources accordingly.
The decline stage is the end of the life cycle. Product sales continue to decline                                                                                                                                       Failure to do so shortens the life cycle
and core market share is at risk. The design focus in this stage is to minimize                                                                                                                                         of a product.
maintenance costs and develop transition strategies to migrate customers to new
products. Testing of the next generation product should begin at this stage.

Consider the life cycle of a product when planning and preparing for the future.
During the introduction phase, work closely with early adopters to refine and tune
products. During the growth stage, focus on scaling product supply and perform-
ance. During the maturity stage, focus on customer satisfaction through perform-
ance enhancements and improved support. During decline, focus on facilitating the
transition to next generation products. Note that the development cycle for the next-
generation product begins during the grOlNlh stage of a current-generation product.

See also Development Cycle. Hierarchy of Needs, Iteration, and Prototyping.
