There are four kinds of redundancy: diverse, homogenous, active, and passive,l               Tomayko, Journal of the British Interplanetary   2 steel guy wires                attach base to crane                     Steel Cras..,; Member                Fabricated Double U-Bolts
                                                                                                                                              3 eyebolts                       attach cape to cow
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                -rl-.....,.,
                                                                                             Sode/y. 1985. vol. 38, p. 545-552.
Diverse redundancy is the use of multiple elements of different types (e.g., use of                                                                                                                               TO'Ner Crane
text, audio, and video to present the same information). Diverse redundancy is
resistant to a single cause of failure, but is complex to implement and maintain.
For example, high-speed trains often have diverse redundancy in their braking
systems-one electric brake, one hydraulic brake, and one pneumatic brake. A                                                                   The Super Cow entry 111 the Houston
single cause is unlikely to resu lt in a cascade failure in ali three braking systems.                                                        Cow Parade 200] had a unique                                                       •
                                                                                                                                              design specification- it was to Sit atop
Homogenous redundancy is the use of multiple elements of a single type (e.g.,                                                                 a thirty-story tower crane for the
use of multiple independent strands to compose a rope). Homogenous redun-                                                                     duration of hUrricane seasoll. Since
dancy is relatively simple to implement and maintain but is susceptible to single                                                             the consequences of Super Cow
causes of failure- i.e., the type of cause that results in failure in one element                                                             laking fligl1t III high winds could be
can result in failure of other redundant elements. For example, a sharp edge that                                                             grave, vallOUS forms of reclundancy
severs one strand of a rope can sever others.                                                                                                 were applied to keep him attached.
                                                                                                                                              Despite many severe thunderstorms
Active redundancy is the application of redundant elements at all times (e.g.,                                                                (wind gusts In excess of 60 MPH),
using multiple independent pillars to support a roof) . Active redundancy guards                                                              Super Cowexpenenced no failure.
against both system and element failure-i.e. , it distributes loads across all ele-                                                           damage, or unintended flights during
ments such that the load on the each element and the overall system is reduced.                                                               his four-month stay on the crane.
Active redundancy also allows for element failure, repair, and substitution with
minimal disruption of system performance_

Passive redundancy is the application of redundant elements only when an active
element fails (e.g., using a spare tire on a vehicle in the event of a flat tire).
Passive redundancy is ideal for noncritical elements, but it will result in system
failure when used for elements critical to system operation. Passive redundancy
is the simplest and most common kind of redundancy.

Use diverse redundancy for critical systems when the probable causes of fai lure
cannot be anticipated. Use homogenous redundancy when the probable ca uses
of failure can be anticipated. Use active redundancy for critical systems that must
maintain stable performance in the event of element failure or extreme changes
in system load. Use passive redundancy for noncritical elements within systems,
or systems In which performance interruptions are tolerable. The four kinds of
redundancy should be used in combination to achieve highly reliable systems.

 See also Factor of Safety, Modularity, Structural Forms, and Weakest Link.


