Sava nna Preference
A tendency to prefer savanna-like environments to otller
types of enVIronments. I


People tend to prefer savanna-like environments-open areas, scattered trees,               I   Also known as savanna hypothesiS.
water, and uniform grassiness-to other natural environments that are simple, such
as desert; dense, such as jungle; or com plex, such as mountains. The preference           - The seminal article on tile savanna preference
is based on the belief that early humans who lived on savannas enjoyed a survival              is "Development 01 Visual Preference lor
                                                                                               Natural Environments" by John D. Balling and
advantage over humans who lived in other environments. This advantage ultimately
                                                                                               John H. Falk1ll, Envlfonment and BehaVIOr,
resulted in the development of a genetic predisposition to favor savanna environ-               1982, vol. }l1, p. 5 28.
ments that stitl manifests itself today. It may be no coincidence that the parks,
resorts, and golf courses of the world all resemble savannas-they may renect an
                                                                                           • See, for example, -Tt'le Biological Basis for
unconscious preference for the look and feel of our east-African ancestral home,l            Human Values of Nature" by Stcphen R.
                                                                                             Kellerl, in The Bioph//IB HypothesiS by Stephen
The characteristics of savannas that people prefer include depth, openness, uniform            R. Kellert and Edward O. Wilson (c(hIOrsl.
grassy coverings, and scattered trees, as opposed to obstructed vielNS, disordered             Island Press, 1993.

high complexity, and rough textures. The preference is found acrpss all age ranges
and cultures, though it is strongest in children and grows 'Neaker with age. This find-
ing is thought to corroborate the evolutionary origin of the preference; j,e" humans
are increasi ngly influenced by knowledge, culture, and experience with other
environments as they grow older, which interferes with innate preferences,

This ca usal explanation has been criticized as recent evidence suggests that early
humans lived in a variety of environments (e.g. closed-canopy woodlands), but
evidence for the existence of the preference is strong. For example, in an experiment
where people were presented with images of savannas, deciduous forests, conifer-
ous forests, rain forests, and desert environments, lush savannas were consistently
preferred over other choices as a place to live or visit. The theory that the preference
is related to the savanna's perceived resource richness and temperate climate is
supported by the finding that the least preferred environment is the arid desert
landscape.!

People have a general landscape preference, independent of culture, for savanna-
like or park-like environments. Consider the savanna preference in the design of
landscapes, advertiSing, and any other design that involves the creation or depiction
of natural environments. The preference is strongest in young child ren . Therefore,
consider savanna-like environments in the design of settings for children's stories
and play environments.

See also Archetypes, Mimicry, and Prospect~Refuge.




