                                                                                              types of symmetries in natural and human-
types of symmetry: reflection, rotation, and translation. 1                                                                                                  Reflection                                 Rotation                               Translation
                                                                                              created forms is Handbook of Regular Patterns
                                                                                              by Peter S. Stevens, MIT Press, 1984.
Reflection symmetry refers to the mirroring of an equivalent elemen t around a
central axis or mirror line. Reflection symmetry can occur in any orientation as          J   See, lor example, -The Status of Minimum
long as the element is the same on ooth sides of the mirror line. Natural forms that          Principle in the TheorelJcal Analysis of Visual   Comblnahons Of symmetries can         ml':OI rofdtes mullJple. complex
grow or move across the Earth's surface have evolved to exhibit reflection symme·             Pefception~ by Gary Hatfteld and William
                                                                                                                                                create harmOniOus. interesting. and   symmeilies III its de!ilgn. resulting in
try. For example, a butterfly exhibits reflection symmetry in its body and wings.             Epstein. Psychological Bullebn, 1985, YOI. 97,
                                                                                                                                                memor~ble de:.lgns. For example,      a structure thaI IS hoth pleaSing and
                                                                                              p. 155--186; and "Facial Resemblance
                                                                                              Enhances Trust- by lisa M. DeBrulne,              Ihe Notre Dame Cathedral              Intefe5tlllg to the eye.
Rotation symmetry refers to the rotation of equivalent elements around a common               Proceedings of The Royal Society: BiologIcal
center. Rotation symmetry can occur at any angle or frequency as long as the                  Sciences, vol. 269(1498), p. 1307·1312.
elements share a common center. Natural forms that grow or move up or down a
perpendicular to the Earth's surface have evolved to exhibit rotation symmetry.
For example, a sunflower exhibits rotation symmetry in both its stem and petals.                                                                                                                                                 Mirror Line

 Translation symmetry refers to the location of equivalent elements in different
areas of space. Translation symmetry can occur in any direction and over any dis·
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 Reflection
tance as long as the basic orientation of the element is maintained. Natural forms
exhibit translation symmetry through repraduction---crealing similar looking off·
spring. For example, a school of fish exhibits translation symmetry across multiple,
independent organisms.2

Aside from Iheir aesthetic properties, symmetric forms have other qualities that are
potentially benerlCial to designers. For example, symmetric forms tend to be seen as                                                                                                                                                                             Translation
figure images rather than ground images, which means they receive more attention
and be better recalled than other elements; symmetric forms are simpler than asym·                                                                                                                                               Angle = IS'
metric farms, which also gives them an advantage with regards to recognition and
recall; and symmetric faces are perceived as more attractive than asymmetric faces.l
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 Rotation

Symmetry is the most basic and enduring aspect of beauty. Use symmetry in
design to convey balance, harmony, and stability. Use simple symmetrical forms
when recognition and reca ll are important, and more complex combinations of the
different types of symmetries when aesthetics and interestingness are important.
