For men, attraction is primarily a function of physica l appearance. Women who                                                                                                              A   R       c             o
                                                                                                1950s and Miss America winners since the            helVe not changed - they have
are underweight or overweight are generally perceived as less attractive, but in all
                                                                                                 1920s, resea rchers discovered that the waisl-     remained constant a1 around 0.90
cases women with waist-to-hip ratios approximating 0.70 are perceived as the most               to-hip ratios remained between 0.68 and 0.72        for men, and 0.70 for women.
attractive for their respective weight group. For women, attraction is a function of both       despite a downward trend in body weight.
physical appearance and financial status. Financial status is biologically important
because it ensures a woman of security and status for herself and her children.
However, as women become increasingly independent with resources of their own,
the strength of financial status as a factor in attraction diminishes. Similarly, women
of modest resources may be attracted to men of low financial status when their
physical characteristics indicate strong male features like dominance and mas-
culinity (e.g., tall stature), but men with both high waist-to-hip ratios and high
financial status are perceived as the most desirable.

The waist-to-hip ratio has design implications for the depiction of the human
form . When the presentation of attractive women is a key element of a design, use
renderings or images of women with waist-to-hip ratios of approximately 0.70. When
the presentation of attractive men is a key element of a design, use renderings or
images of men with waist-to-hip ratios of approximately 0.90, strong male features,
and visible indicators of wealth or status (e.g., expensive cloth ing) .

See also Attractiveness Bias, Baby-Face Bias, and Golden Ratio.




