Wea kest Li nk
The deliberate use of a weak elemen t thaI will fail in order
to protect other elements in the system from damage.


It is said that a chain is only as strong as its weakest link. This suggests that the
weakest link in a chain is also the least valuable and most expendable link-a
liability to the system that should be reinforced, replaced, or removed, However,
the weakest element in a system can be used to protect other more important
elements, essentially making the weakest link one of the most important elements
in the system. For example, electrical circuits are protected by fuses, which are
designed to fail so that a power surge doesn't damage the circuit. The fuse is
the weakest link in the system. As such, the fuse is also the most valuable link
in the system.

The weakest link in a system can function in one of two ways: it can fail and
passively minimize damage, or it can fail and activate additional systems that
actively minimize damage. An example of a passive design is the use of fuses
in electrical circuits, where the fuse passively minimizes damage to other com-
ponents on the circuit. An example of an active design is the use of automatic
sprinklers in a building. Sprinkler systems are typically activated by components
that fail to moderate heat-a solder link that melts and activates the sprinkler, or
liquid in a glass cell that expands to brea k the glass when heated-which then
activate the release of the water.

Applying the weakest-link principle involves several steps: identifying a failure
condition; identifying or defining the weakest link in the system for that failure
condition; further weakening the weakest link and strengthening the other links
as necessary to address the failure condition; and ensuring that the weakest link
will only fail under the appropriate, predefined failure conditions. The weakest-
link principle is limited to systems in which a particular failure condition affects
multiple elements in the system. Systems with decentralized and disconnected
elements cannot benefit from the principle since the elements of the system are
not links in any kind of chain.
                                                                                                                                                       -
The weakest link in a system exists by design or by default- either way, it is
always present. Therefore. consider the weakest link principle when designing
systems in which failures affect multiple elements. Use the weakest link to shut        Crumple zones are one of Ihe           tile passenger shell The passenger
down the system or activate other protective systems. Perform adequate testing          rnost significant automobile sa fety   shell is reinforced to bettel protect
